# TAPEOUT — the Verilog dojo

A first-person browser RPG that teaches Verilog. The retired Claude artifact
constraint no longer requires a monolith: game code is organized by subsystem
under `src/`, with `src/tapeout.jsx` retained as the stable default-export entry.

## Hard constraints — do not violate

1. **Pedagogy is canonical.** Do not casually rewrite challenge specifications,
   test vectors, field-note content, or station learning order.
2. **three.js is pinned to r185.** `three/addons/*` is allowed. Future rendering
   upgrades remain standalone migrations with their own gate and visual review.
3. **Save compatibility is permanent.** Game modules use the async
   `window.storage` API. Browser `localStorage` belongs only in the local shim
   in `src/main.jsx`; do not redesign the save schema during unrelated work.
4. **All audio remains synthesized** through Web Audio.
5. **Visual assets are allowed** when their source/license is documented and
   they meet the project performance budget.
6. **lucide-react icons are imported by name.** Alias `Map as MapIcon` because
   it collides with the global.
7. **No new runtime dependencies without a standalone proposal.** Current
   runtime packages are react, react-dom, three, and lucide-react.

## Workflow

- Run `npm run gate` after every meaningful change. It must print `GATE GREEN`.
- The gate covers bundling, dependency/storage compatibility, fail-closed unit
  tests, all-world layout/reachability, canonical content, real three.js scene
  construction/progress synchronization, and React smoke behavior.
- The historical 309-check scripts were never committed. The checked-in gate is
  explicitly reconstructed coverage; strengthen it without claiming otherwise.
- Gate scripts copy the complete `src/` module graph into `.gate/` and append
  test-only exports to the copied entry. Never add test-only exports to runtime
  modules.
- Use `npm test`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run dev` for focused work.

## Navigation

- `src/engine/` — compiler, simulator, netlists, diagnostics, RTL export
- `src/game/` — canonical content, RPG systems, recall scheduling
- `src/world/` — collision, progression, layouts, pure world models
- `src/graphics/` — three.js primitives, post FX, creatures, world builders
- `src/audio/` — synthesized SFX, tracks, and music state machine
- `src/ui/` — shared components and screen families
- `src/app/` — save contracts and App shell
- `src/telemetry/` — flight recorder
- `dev/` and `tests/` — gate harnesses and focused tests

## Gotchas learned the hard way

- CodeScreen and Forge have similar execution pipelines with intentional
  differences. Test both when changing compile/run behavior.
- World layouts rasterize at 2-unit cell centers. Overall spans must align with
  the raster, but some canonical gallery spurs intentionally use odd edges.
  Treat full station/boss BFS—not a blanket parity rule—as authoritative.
- Gate colliders must span the full corridor opening.
- Station `ord` values must agree between 3D and 2D views and derive from the
  canonical `challengesOf()` ordering.
- Transparent/additive meshes must not cast shadows.
- GLSL needs browser verification in addition to the headless gate.
